      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey can u help me with physics? im studying for my circuits final. can u explain the difference between how a capacitor acts in a dc circuit vs an ac circuit? also i keep forgetting the formula for capacitive reactance so if u could include that would be great thanks",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Absolutely! In a DC circuit, a capacitor eventually charges up to the voltage of the source and acts like an open circuit after reaching its fully charged state. In an AC circuit, however, a capacitor continuously charges and discharges in response to the alternating nature of the voltage. The formula for capacitive reactance, which is the resistance offered by a capacitor to AC, is Xc = 1/(2πfC), where Xc is capacitive reactance, f is the frequency of the AC, and C is the capacitance.",
